Next.js App Router represents a significant evolution in the Next.js framework, introducing a new routing system based on the file system. This guide covers everything you need to know about App Router, from basic concepts to advanced patterns.

## Key Features

- **File-based routing**: Routes are defined by the folder structure
- **Server Components**: Components that render on the server by default
- **Streaming**: Progressive loading of page content
- **Nested layouts**: Shared UI between routes

## Getting Started

To use App Router, you need to create an `app` directory in your project root. This directory will contain all your routes and layouts.

```jsx
// app/page.tsx
export default function Home() {
  return <h1>Welcome to Next.js!</h1>
}
```

## Layouts

Layouts allow you to share UI between multiple pages. Create a `layout.tsx` file in your app directory:

```jsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

This is just the beginning of what you can do with Next.js App Router. The possibilities are endless!

Performance optimization is crucial for creating smooth, responsive React applications. In this comprehensive guide, we'll explore various techniques to improve your app's performance.

## Memoization

React provides several memoization techniques to prevent unnecessary re-renders:

### React.memo

```jsx
const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {
  return <div>{data}</div>
})
```

### useMemo

```jsx
const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {
  return computeExpensiveValue(a, b)
}, [a, b])
```

### useCallback

```jsx
const memoizedCallback = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(a, b)
}, [a, b])
```

## Code Splitting

Split your code to load only what's needed:

```jsx
const LazyComponent = React.lazy(() => import('./LazyComponent'))

function App() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <LazyComponent />
    </Suspense>
  )
}
```

## Virtual Scrolling

For large lists, implement virtual scrolling to render only visible items:

```jsx
import { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window'

const VirtualList = ({ items }) => (
  <List
    height={600}
    itemCount={items.length}
    itemSize={50}
  >
    {({ index, style }) => (
      <div style={style}>
        {items[index]}
      </div>
    )}
  </List>
)
```

These techniques will significantly improve your React application's performance!

CSS Grid and Flexbox are both powerful layout systems, but they serve different purposes. Understanding when to use each can make your CSS more efficient and maintainable.

## CSS Grid

CSS Grid is designed for two-dimensional layouts. Use it when you need to control both rows and columns.

### When to use Grid:
- Complex layouts with multiple rows and columns
- When you need precise control over item placement
- Creating card layouts or dashboard interfaces
- When items need to span multiple rows/columns

```css
.grid-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
  gap: 20px;
}

.grid-item {
  grid-column: span 2;
  grid-row: span 1;
}
```

## Flexbox

Flexbox is designed for one-dimensional layouts. Use it for arranging items in a single direction.

### When to use Flexbox:
- Aligning items in a single row or column
- Distributing space between items
- Creating responsive navigation bars
- Centering content

```css
.flex-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

.flex-item {
  flex: 1 1 200px;
}
```

## Best Practices

1. **Use Grid for page layouts** - Header, main content, sidebar, footer
2. **Use Flexbox for components** - Navigation bars, card content, form layouts
3. **Combine both** - Use Grid for the overall layout and Flexbox for individual components

```css
.page-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header header"
    "sidebar main"
    "footer footer";
}

.nav-bar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
}
```

Choose the right tool for the job, and your layouts will be more efficient and maintainable!

TypeScript brings type safety to React development, but it's important to use it effectively. Here are the best practices that will make your code more maintainable and less error-prone.

## Type Definitions

### Component Props

```tsx
interface ButtonProps {
  children: React.ReactNode
  onClick: () => void
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary'
  disabled?: boolean
}

const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ 
  children, 
  onClick, 
  variant = 'primary', 
  disabled = false 
}) => {
  return (
    <button 
      className={`btn btn-${variant}`}
      onClick={onClick}
      disabled={disabled}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  )
}
```

### State Types

```tsx
interface User {
  id: number
  name: string
  email: string
  avatar?: string
}

const UserProfile: React.FC = () => {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null)
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(false)

  // ...
}
```

## Generic Components

```tsx
interface ListProps<T> {
  items: T[]
  renderItem: (item: T) => React.ReactNode
  keyExtractor: (item: T) => string
}

function List<T>({ items, renderItem, keyExtractor }: ListProps<T>) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={keyExtractor(item)}>
          {renderItem(item)}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}

// Usage
<List
  items={users}
  renderItem={(user) => <UserCard user={user} />}
  keyExtractor={(user) => user.id.toString()}
/>
```

## Event Handlers

```tsx
const handleSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
  e.preventDefault()
  // Handle form submission
}

const handleInputChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
  setValue(e.target.value)
}

const handleClick = (e: React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) => {
  // Handle click
}
```

## Custom Hooks

```tsx
interface UseApiResult<T> {
  data: T | null
  loading: boolean
  error: string | null
  refetch: () => void
}

function useApi<T>(url: string): UseApiResult<T> {
  const [data, setData] = useState<T | null>(null)
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true)
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null)

  const fetchData = useCallback(async () => {
    try {
      setLoading(true)
      const response = await fetch(url)
      const result = await response.json()
      setData(result)
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'An error occurred')
    } finally {
      setLoading(false)
    }
  }, [url])

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchData()
  }, [fetchData])

  return { data, loading, error, refetch: fetchData }
}
```

## Utility Types

```tsx
// Partial for optional updates
interface UserUpdate extends Partial<User> {
  id: number
}

// Pick for selecting specific properties
interface UserSummary extends Pick<User, 'id' | 'name'> {}

// Omit for excluding properties
interface NewUser extends Omit<User, 'id'> {}
```

These practices will help you write more robust and maintainable TypeScript React applications!
